\]
be an event package. If \(\mathfrak{E}\) admits an admissible global realization \((\Omega,\{\pi_c\}_{c\in\mathcal{C}})\), then:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item every context atom is covered by some \(\omega\in\Omega\);
    \item every context event is realized as an inverse image under the corresponding \(\pi_c\);
    \item every accepted shared-event identification is globally respected;
    \item every exact admissible backend built from the realized global atoms is feasible.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, no accepted-proposal exact obstruction can occur when an admissible global realization exists.

source_wording_tex:
\label{def:duality_confinement:involutive-object-ledger}
An \emph{involutive object ledger} is a tuple
\[
  \IOL=(X,J,\mu,\psi,Y,\Jiso)
\]
consisting of the following data:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item a visible carrier \(X\);
  \item an involution \(J:X\to X\), so \(J(Jx)=x\) for every
    \(x\in X\);
  \item a positive measure, or in the finite case a positive weight
    system, \(\mu\) on \(X\);
  \item a real Hilbert response space \(Y\);
  \item a linear isometric involution \(\Jiso:Y\to Y\);
  \item a measurable readout \(\psi:X\to Y\) satisfying the
    equivariance condition
    \[
      \psi(Jx)=\Jiso\psi(x)
      \qquad (x\in X).
    \]
\end{enumerate}
The fixed locus of the ledger involution is
\[
  \Fix(J)=\{x\in X: Jx=x\}.
\]

expanded_source_wording_tex:
\label{def:duality_confinement:involutive-object-ledger}
An \emph{involutive object ledger} is a tuple
\[
  \hat{\mathfrak{X}}=(X,J,\mu,\psi,Y,J_{\mathrm{iso}})
\]
consisting of the following data:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item a visible carrier \(X\);
  \item an involution \(J:X\to X\), so \(J(Jx)=x\) for every
    \(x\in X\);
  \item a positive measure, or in the finite case a positive weight
    system, \(\mu\) on \(X\);
  \item a real Hilbert response space \(Y\);
  \item a linear isometric involution \(J_{\mathrm{iso}}:Y\to Y\);
  \item a measurable readout \(\psi:X\to Y\) satisfying the
    equivariance condition
    \[
      \psi(Jx)=J_{\mathrm{iso}}\psi(x)
      \qquad (x\in X).
    \]
\end{enumerate}
The fixed locus of the ledger involution is
\[
  \Fix(J)=\{x\in X: Jx=x\}.
\]

source_wording_tex:
The paper proceeds as follows. Section~\ref{sec:primitives-rubric} introduces the primitive framework and the canonical class rubric. Section~\ref{sec:observables-evidence} defines the operational observables and evidence discipline. Section~\ref{sec:class-i-ii} presents the Class-I / Class-II separation and its no-fake-arrow validation. Section~\ref{sec:class-iii-iv} establishes the P4-active classes. Section~\ref{sec:observable-map-support} places all four classes in a common observable-space map and summarizes the supporting capacity and upstream-bridge results. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses scope, limitations, and what remains open. The main conclusion is that layer birth is not one phenomenon but a small family of primitive-activation events, and that the experimentally accessible low-complexity cases already support a four-class observable taxonomy.
 \section{Primitive framework and canonical class rubric}
\label{sec:primitives-rubric}

